  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching a basic Island instead of Tundra is suboptimal here. With Swords to Plowshares in hand, having access to white mana is valuable. Tundra provides both blue and white mana, enabling Daze (as a blue source that can be returned) AND Swords to Plowshares. A basic Island only provides blue. Additionally, with two Wastelands in hand, the player has strong mana denial tools - having their own mana on a dual land that covers both colors is more resilient to their own mana constraints. The downside of Tundra (vulnerability to opponent's Wasteland) is real but relatively minor compared to the upside of having white mana access, especially since they have two Wastelands of their own and can fetch basics later.",
      "action_taken": "Fetched a basic Island from Flooded Strand",
      "better_line": "Fetch Tundra to have access to both blue mana for Daze/Force of Will and white mana for Swords to Plowshares, maximizing flexibility with the current hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 9,
      "player": "Sonnet Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 9,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Using Force of Will to counter a Daze on a Thundertrap Trainer (a 1/2 creature) is a significant overcommitment of resources. Force of Will costs a card from hand plus 1 life, making it a 2-for-1 against yourself. The Thundertrap Trainer is a low-impact creature - just a 1/2 that cantrips. The opponent only spent a free Daze (returning an Island) to counter it, meaning they invested very little. MiMo Stoic could have simply let the Daze resolve and lost only the Trainer, then replayed it next turn or deployed other threats. Instead, they're down Force of Will plus an exiled blue card (likely Brainstorm or Stern Scolding) to resolve a 1/2 creature. This leaves them without their best counterspell for the rest of the game against whatever threats the opponent presents.",
      "action_taken": "Used Force of Will (alternative cost: exile a blue card + pay 1 life) to counter Daze targeting Thundertrap Trainer.",
      "better_line": "Let Daze resolve and counter the Thundertrap Trainer. Replay Trainer next turn when you have more mana to pay for Daze, or hold Force of Will for a more impactful threat from the opponent. Paying 1 life into Daze was also an option since both Islands were tapped, but simply accepting the tempo loss would preserve Force of Will for critical moments.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 18,
      "player": "MiMo Stoic",
      "decision_index": 23,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Using Force of Will (exiling a blue card + 1 life) to counter a Daze protecting a 1/2 Thundertrap Trainer is a massive overcommitment of resources. Force of Will is a 2-for-1 card disadvantage to protect a low-impact creature. MiMo Stoic has a second Thundertrap Trainer in hand and could simply replay it next turn, preserving Force of Will for a much more impactful counterspell battle.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Force of Will using alternative cost (exile blue card + 1 life) to counter Daze and protect Thundertrap Trainer",
      "better_line": "Let Daze resolve and counter the Thundertrap Trainer. Save Force of Will for a critical spell. Cast the second Thundertrap Trainer next turn when you have mana to pay for Daze if needed, or hold up Stern Scolding as interaction.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 20,
