      "decision_index": 62,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sonnet Vorthos declined to cast Swords to Plowshares on the attacking Snapcaster Mage (which is tapped, indicating it attacked). Snapcaster Mage is a 2/1 that already provided its ETB value (likely flashing back Stern Scolding or similar), and removing it now costs only W mana (Tundra is untapped) while preventing future damage. More importantly, removing Snapcaster Mage puts another instant in the graveyard, which helps fuel Murktide Regent's delve. With 6 cards already in graveyard, removing Snapcaster now and having 7 cards in graveyard (plus lands potentially) makes casting Murktide much easier. The opponent also has Volcanic Island meaning they have red mana for potential burn/removal, so racing is important.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority during combat without using Swords to Plowshares on the tapped Snapcaster Mage.",
      "better_line": "Cast Swords to Plowshares targeting Snapcaster Mage using Tundra. This removes the threat, adds to graveyard count for Murktide Regent, and the 2 life gained by opponent is negligible compared to the continued damage and board presence of Snapcaster.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 64,
      "player": "Sonnet Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 64,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sonnet Vorthos declined to use Wasteland to destroy opponent's Volcanic Island (their only red source) or cast Swords to Plowshares on the tapped Snapcaster Mage. The Snapcaster Mage is a meaningful threat that already provided value (likely flashing back Stern Scolding to counter Phelia based on the graveyard state). More importantly, Swords to Plowshares should be cast on the Snapcaster Mage while it's tapped and the opponent has no untapped mana to protect it - waiting gives the opponent a chance to untap and hold up countermagic. Wasteland on Volcanic Island would also cut off the opponent's red mana, potentially stranding cards in hand.",
